  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This eLife article has an early but clear scholarly footprint: it has been cited twice in OpenAlex, reused in three documented downstream contexts, and attracted one scite mention. The record suggests immediate uptake within related chromatin and reproductive biology research, while broader attention remains modest so far.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published in 2023 in eLife, <em>Chromosome-specific maturation of the epigenome in the Drosophila male germline</em> addresses how chromatin features evolve during spermatogenesis, with particular focus on sex chromosomes and dosage-related regulation in the male germline. The paper sits at the intersection of chromatin biology, epigenetics, germline development, and dosage compensation, and it is supported by openly available data deposited in Dryad.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">In the citation record, the article has received two citations, both appearing in 2025. One is a preprint on chromosome-specific chromatin landscapes and DNA replication programs in Drosophila testis tumor stem cell-like cells, and the other is a journal article in <em>Molecular Biology and Evolution</em> on chromatin landscape and dosage compensation in <em>Artemia franciscana</em>. That pattern points to a niche but relevant scholarly audience engaging with the paper\u2019s chromatin-and-sex-chromosome framework.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Beyond direct citations, the work shows three documented reuse signals, including one article, one preprint, and one biomedical article mention in Europe PMC. These signals reinforce that the paper is being taken up as a reference point in adjacent studies of sperm and testicular function, reproductive biology, chromatin, and dosage compensation rather than remaining isolated within a single subfield.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The surrounding attention record is otherwise quiet. There are no recorded online mentions in the available Event Data, and the preprint metrics archive is unavailable, so the current narrative is driven mainly by citations and reuse rather than broader public discussion. The article\u2019s open-access status and linked peer review materials may also support discoverability and reuse over time.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Overall, the article\u2019s impact profile is best described as early-stage but substantive: limited in volume, yet already embedded in subsequent work on chromosome-specific chromatin regulation and reproductive epigenetics. The available evidence shows focused scholarly uptake, with room for the citation network to develop further as the field continues to build on these findings.</p>",
